# 12.8 Payment Management

The application must let Finance record payment confirmation for fully approved expense requests. Payment execution remains outside the application; this feature records the result and its supporting evidence.

### Payment queue

Finance can view requests with status `Approved — Awaiting Payment`. The queue may be an `Awaiting Payment` tab within Requests or Approvals rather than a separate module.

Required columns:

* request ID;
* request title;
* project;
* department;
* recipient;
* approved amount;
* required date;
* days awaiting payment;
* payment status.

Finance can open a request, filter by project, department, or required date, and select `Record Payment`.

### Payment confirmation preconditions

The action is available only when:

* the request has received Department Manager approval;
* the request has received Finance approval;
* the request status is `Approved — Awaiting Payment`;
* the current role is Finance Reviewer.

### Payment form

Required fields:

* payment date;
* paid amount;
* payment method;
* beneficiary or recipient name;
* destination bank or payment channel;
* transfer reference number;
* transfer-proof attachment.

Optional fields:

* bank-account suffix;
* payment notes;
* internal voucher number.

The prototype must not require a full bank-account number. Supported payment methods are Bank Transfer, Virtual Account, Corporate Card, and Other. Bank Transfer is used in the main demo.

### Payment validation

The system must:

* allow payment confirmation only for fully approved requests;
* require transfer proof for Bank Transfer;
* prevent a paid amount of zero or less;
* prevent a paid amount greater than the approved amount;
* warn and require a Finance comment when an entered amount differs from the approved amount;
* block completion for a mismatched amount in the one-day prototype;
* prevent a request from being paid twice;
* record the confirming user and timestamp.

Partial payments are out of scope for the one-day prototype, so `Paid Amount = Approved Amount`.

Example:

* Before payment: allocation Rp800M, actual spending Rp200M, approved but unpaid commitments Rp150M, available budget Rp450M.
* After confirming the Rp150M payment: actual spending Rp350M, approved but unpaid commitments Rp0, available budget remains Rp450M.

Payment confirmation changes the classification of the amount; it does not reduce available budget a second time.

### Transfer-proof behavior

The upload is a mock evidence flow. It may accept a local PDF, PNG, JPG, or JPEG, display filename, type, and size, show a preview placeholder, and allow replacement before submission. Real file storage and download are not required.

### Payment effect

After confirmation:

* the request status becomes `Paid`;
* the commitment decreases by the paid amount;
* actual spending increases by the same amount;
* available budget is not reduced a second time;
* project, department, and annual-budget summaries update consistently;
* payment details become read-only;
* the payment event is added to the audit history.

The confirmation step should show:

> This action will mark the request as paid and move the approved amount from committed funds to actual spending.

After payment, the request detail displays payment date, paid amount, method, beneficiary, destination bank or channel, transfer reference, transfer-proof filename, Finance processor, and processing timestamp.

