## 20. Business Rules

1. A project must have at least one project allocation.

2. A project allocation must belong to one department and one budget line.

3. The sum of project allocations under a budget line cannot exceed the budget line’s available amount.

4. An expense request must reference one project allocation.

5. A request cannot be submitted if its amount exceeds the project allocation’s available budget.

6. Final approval changes the request to `Approved — Awaiting Payment` and increases approved but unpaid commitments.

7. Rejected or revision-required requests do not affect commitments or actual spending.

8. Payment confirmation converts the commitment into actual spending and reduces the unpaid commitment by the same amount.

9. Payment confirmation must not reduce available budget a second time.

10. A project owner cannot automatically spend from another department’s allocation.

11. Department budget owners can approve only requests charged to their department.

12. Finance can review requests across all departments and is the only role that can record payment.

13. Transfer proof is mandatory for bank-transfer payments.

14. The paid amount must equal the approved amount in the prototype; partial payments are out of scope.

15. A request may be paid only once, and paid requests cannot be edited.

16. Payment confirmation must record the Finance user, timestamp, payment details, and transfer-proof filename in the audit history.

17. Paid amount and status must reconcile across the project, department, and annual-budget views.

18. One financial request is stored once even when it appears in multiple reporting views.

